      "math_academy_id": "q-335018",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-0.5x+0.5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.2,\\,1]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is\n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-0.5x+0.5xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.5x+0.5xy)=-0.5+0.5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.5x+0.5xy)=0.5x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-5xy)=1-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5+0.5y & 0.5x \\\\ -5y & 1-5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5+0.5(1) & 0.5(0.2) \\\\ -5(1) & 1-5(0.2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.1 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0.1 \\\\ -5 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-(-5)\\cdot 0.1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source).",
